// AUTHORED BATCH 001 — written by Claude, target words drawn from the CEFR-J list.
//
// STATUS: mechanically validated (stage 3) but NOT blind-checked (stage 4).
// Do not ship these to users until a second model has solved them without the
// key. The failure these questions are most likely to carry is two defensible
// answers, and only stage 4 catches it.
{"target_word": "emerge", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "After weeks of negotiation, a compromise finally began to ______ from the discussions between both sides.", "correct_answer": "emerge", "distractors": ["vanish", "purchase", "rehearse"], "explanation": "Weeks of negotiation ending in a compromise means something gradually came into view, which is what 'emerge' describes. 'Vanish' reverses that, and neither purchasing nor rehearsing is something a compromise can do.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "effective", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "The new safety procedures proved highly ______, reducing workplace injuries by almost half.", "correct_answer": "effective", "distractors": ["fictional", "edible", "alphabetical"], "explanation": "Halving injuries is evidence that the procedures worked, which is what 'effective' means. The other three describe properties a set of procedures could technically have, but none of them would be demonstrated by a drop in injuries.", "difficulty_est": 2}
{"target_word": "decrease", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "Analysts were surprised by the sharp ______ in fuel consumption following the new tax.", "correct_answer": "decrease", "distractors": ["apology", "ceremony", "portrait"], "explanation": "A tax on fuel would reduce how much is used, and 'decrease' names that fall. The other options are things that cannot be 'in fuel consumption' at all.", "difficulty_est": 2}
{"target_word": "ashamed", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "He felt deeply ______ of his behaviour and apologised to everyone the following morning.", "correct_answer": "ashamed", "distractors": ["proud", "fond", "unaware"], "explanation": "The apology tells you he judged his own behaviour badly, which is what 'ashamed' means. 'Proud' and 'fond' would contradict the apology, and someone unaware of their behaviour would have no reason to apologise for it.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "previous", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "The report contradicts several findings published in the ______ study by the same research team.", "correct_answer": "previous", "distractors": ["edible", "circular", "audible"], "explanation": "A report can only contradict findings that already exist, so the study must be an earlier one — 'previous'. The other three are adjectives that cannot sensibly describe a research study here.", "difficulty_est": 2}
{"target_word": "crisis", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "The government was criticised for responding slowly to the housing ______ in the northern cities.", "correct_answer": "crisis", "distractors": ["recipe", "portrait", "melody"], "explanation": "Something a government is criticised for responding slowly to must be a serious problem, which 'crisis' names. None of the others is something that would require a government response.", "difficulty_est": 2}
{"target_word": "attract", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "The festival was designed to ______ visitors from outside the region during the quiet winter months.", "correct_answer": "attract", "distractors": ["memorise", "translate", "inherit"], "explanation": "A festival planned for a quiet season exists to bring people in, which is what 'attract' means. You cannot memorise, translate or inherit visitors.", "difficulty_est": 2}
{"target_word": "proof", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "The lawyer argued that suspicion alone was not sufficient ______ of any wrongdoing by her client.", "correct_answer": "proof", "distractors": ["rehearsal", "furniture", "laughter"], "explanation": "Setting suspicion against what is 'sufficient' points at evidence, which is what 'proof' means. The others cannot be 'of wrongdoing'.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "spoil", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "A single careless remark can ______ an evening that everyone else had been enjoying.", "correct_answer": "spoil", "distractors": ["measure", "translate", "inherit"], "explanation": "The contrast with everyone else enjoying it shows the remark ruins the evening, which is what 'spoil' means. Measuring, translating or inheriting an evening makes no sense.", "difficulty_est": 2}
{"target_word": "ordinary", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "What began as an ______ Tuesday turned into the most memorable day of her career.", "correct_answer": "ordinary", "distractors": ["imaginary", "electrical", "edible"], "explanation": "'Turned into the most memorable day' sets up a contrast with something unremarkable, which is what 'ordinary' means. A Tuesday cannot be imaginary, electrical or edible in any ordinary reading.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "device", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "Engineers developed a small ______ that measures air quality and sends readings to a phone.", "correct_answer": "device", "distractors": ["rumour", "holiday", "apology"], "explanation": "Something engineers develop that measures and transmits is a piece of equipment — a 'device'. None of the other options can measure anything.", "difficulty_est": 2}
{"target_word": "arise", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "Disagreements inevitably ______ when two departments are given responsibility for the same budget.", "correct_answer": "arise", "distractors": ["rehearse", "translate", "inherit"], "explanation": "Overlapping responsibility causes disagreements to come about, which 'arise' describes. Disagreements are not things that rehearse, translate or inherit.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "bold", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "The decision to close every branch at once was a ______ move that surprised the industry.", "correct_answer": "bold", "distractors": ["damp", "edible", "audible"], "explanation": "Closing every branch at once is drastic and risky, and it surprised people — that is a 'bold' move. The other adjectives cannot describe a business decision.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "cultivate", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "The charity worked for years to ______ relationships with donors in several different countries.", "correct_answer": "cultivate", "distractors": ["abolish", "memorise", "translate"], "explanation": "Years of work on relationships means developing them carefully over time, which is what 'cultivate' means here. 'Abolish' would end them, and relationships are not memorised or translated.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "glance", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "A single ______ at the figures was enough to tell her something had gone badly wrong.", "correct_answer": "glance", "distractors": ["recipe", "apology", "souvenir"], "explanation": "Something that takes only a moment and is done 'at the figures' is a quick look — a 'glance'. The other three cannot be directed at figures.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "wound", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "The nurse cleaned the ______ carefully before applying a fresh bandage to his arm.", "correct_answer": "wound", "distractors": ["rumour", "melody", "alphabet"], "explanation": "Something cleaned before a bandage is applied is an injury, which 'wound' names. Nothing else listed can be bandaged.", "difficulty_est": 2}
{"target_word": "dramatic", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "There has been a ______ change in how people book holidays since the pandemic ended.", "correct_answer": "dramatic", "distractors": ["edible", "audible", "alphabetical"], "explanation": "A change worth reporting since a major event is a large and striking one, which 'dramatic' describes. A change cannot be edible, audible or alphabetical here.", "difficulty_est": 2}
{"target_word": "lecture", "cefr": "B1", "sentence": "Fewer than twenty students attended the final ______ of what had been a popular course.", "correct_answer": "lecture", "distractors": ["recipe", "souvenir", "bandage"], "explanation": "Something students attend as part of a course is a teaching session — a 'lecture'. None of the other options is something a course consists of.", "difficulty_est": 2}
{"target_word": "subside", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Doctors advised waiting until the swelling had begun to ______ before attempting any further treatment.", "correct_answer": "subside", "distractors": ["rehearse", "inherit", "translate"], "explanation": "You wait for swelling to go down before treating further, and 'subside' means to decrease. Swelling cannot rehearse, inherit or translate.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "underestimate", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Planners tend to ______ how long large infrastructure projects will actually take to complete.", "correct_answer": "underestimate", "distractors": ["rehearse", "translate", "memorise"], "explanation": "The word 'actually' signals that reality exceeds the prediction, so planners judge the time as less than it is — 'underestimate'. The others describe things you cannot do to a duration.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "irrational", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Her fear of bridges was entirely ______, since she knew perfectly well they were safe.", "correct_answer": "irrational", "distractors": ["audible", "edible", "alphabetical"], "explanation": "A fear held despite knowing there is no danger is one without reason, which is what 'irrational' means. A fear cannot be audible, edible or alphabetical.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "barrier", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Language remains the greatest ______ to employment for many recently arrived immigrants.", "correct_answer": "barrier", "distractors": ["recipe", "souvenir", "melody"], "explanation": "Something standing in the way of employment is an obstacle, which 'barrier' names. Nothing else listed can block access to work.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "enhance", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Adding natural light to the workspace did much to ______ both productivity and general morale.", "correct_answer": "enhance", "distractors": ["abolish", "translate", "memorise"], "explanation": "Something that 'did much' for productivity and morale improved them, which is what 'enhance' means. 'Abolish' would remove them entirely, and neither can be translated or memorised.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "welfare", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "The inspectors were primarily concerned with the ______ of the animals kept at the facility.", "correct_answer": "welfare", "distractors": ["alphabet", "souvenir", "rehearsal"], "explanation": "What inspectors check about animals is their health and treatment, which 'welfare' covers. Animals do not have an alphabet, souvenir or rehearsal.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "suppress", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "The regime tried for decades to ______ any reporting that questioned its official version of events.", "correct_answer": "suppress", "distractors": ["rehearse", "inherit", "translate"], "explanation": "A regime acting against reporting that contradicts it is trying to stop that reporting, which 'suppress' means. Rehearsing, inheriting or translating the reporting would not serve that purpose.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "gloomy", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "The forecast for the region's economy remained ______ despite a small rise in exports.", "correct_answer": "gloomy", "distractors": ["edible", "audible", "alphabetical"], "explanation": "'Despite' signals that the small good news did not change a negative outlook, which 'gloomy' describes. A forecast cannot be edible, audible or alphabetical.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "dominate", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Three companies now ______ the market so completely that smaller competitors cannot survive.", "correct_answer": "dominate", "distractors": ["rehearse", "translate", "inherit"], "explanation": "Controlling a market to the point of excluding others is what 'dominate' means. You do not rehearse or translate a market, and inheriting one would not explain competitors failing.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "portion", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Only a small ______ of the funds raised actually reached the communities they were meant for.", "correct_answer": "portion", "distractors": ["melody", "rehearsal", "souvenir"], "explanation": "'Of the funds' calls for a word meaning a part of a whole, which is 'portion'. The other three cannot be a quantity of money.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "obedient", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "The training programme produced dogs that were remarkably ______ even in crowded public spaces.", "correct_answer": "obedient", "distractors": ["edible", "alphabetical", "waterproof"], "explanation": "What training produces in dogs, and what would be notable in a crowd, is that they follow commands — 'obedient'. The others describe properties training could not instil.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "broaden", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "The university hopes to ______ access to its courses by offering more of them online.", "correct_answer": "broaden", "distractors": ["rehearse", "inherit", "memorise"], "explanation": "Putting courses online lets more people reach them, so access is widened — 'broaden'. Access is not something you rehearse, inherit or memorise.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "scandal", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "The accounting ______ cost the firm both its reputation and most of its largest clients.", "correct_answer": "scandal", "distractors": ["souvenir", "rehearsal", "melody"], "explanation": "Something involving accounting that destroys a firm's reputation is a public wrongdoing — a 'scandal'. None of the others would cost a firm its clients.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "weaken", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Repeated flooding had begun to ______ the foundations of several buildings along the river.", "correct_answer": "weaken", "distractors": ["rehearse", "translate", "memorise"], "explanation": "Water damaging foundations over time makes them less strong, which 'weaken' means. Foundations cannot be rehearsed, translated or memorised.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "convinced", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "She remained ______ that the original diagnosis had been wrong, despite what the specialists said.", "correct_answer": "convinced", "distractors": ["edible", "audible", "alphabetical"], "explanation": "Holding a belief against expert disagreement means being firmly certain, which 'convinced' expresses. A person cannot be edible, audible or alphabetical in this sense.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "journalism", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Careful investigative ______ brought the practice to public attention long before regulators acted.", "correct_answer": "journalism", "distractors": ["furniture", "cutlery", "laundry"], "explanation": "'Investigative' and bringing something to public attention both point at reporting, which is 'journalism'. The other three are uncountable nouns that cannot investigate anything.", "difficulty_est": 3}
{"target_word": "prime", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Location was the ______ consideration when the company chose where to open its first store.", "correct_answer": "prime", "distractors": ["edible", "audible", "waterproof"], "explanation": "The consideration that mattered most when choosing a site is the chief one, which 'prime' means. A consideration cannot be edible, audible or waterproof.", "difficulty_est": 4}
{"target_word": "commute", "cefr": "B2", "sentence": "Thousands of workers ______ into the city each morning from towns up to fifty kilometres away.", "correct_answer": "commute", "distractors": ["rehearse", "translate", "memorise"], "explanation": "Travelling into a city for work each morning is exactly what 'commute' means. The other verbs do not describe movement at all.", "difficulty_est": 3}
